WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.230 --> 00:00:06.530
XIAOWEN XIN: Hi, everyone.

00:00:06.530 --> 00:00:08.170
My name is Xiaowen.

00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:10.560
I'm the PM for
Anthos multi-cloud.

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:12.970
And I'm really glad to
have the opportunity

00:00:12.970 --> 00:00:15.490
to be here virtually
with all of you today

00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:19.530
to talk about modernizing and
managing your apps with Anthos.

00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:21.640
A pain point that
we consistently

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:26.410
hear from customers is that
they have a lot of legacy apps,

00:00:26.410 --> 00:00:30.130
running on older infrastructure,
using older frameworks,

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:34.530
and that have become
hard to maintain.

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:36.840
Customers are looking
for a platform

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:40.350
to help modernize these
older apps, as well

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:44.983
as leverage the modern best
practices for their new apps.

00:00:44.983 --> 00:00:46.400
And this is where
Anthos comes in.

00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:52.840
Anthos is an application
modernization platform

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:57.370
that helps teams move
faster and at scale.

00:00:57.370 --> 00:01:01.380
With Anthos, you can modernize
your apps in place, on prem,

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:03.650
or in the cloud.

00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:05.319
You can automate
policy and security

00:01:05.319 --> 00:01:08.290
at scale for all of your apps.

00:01:08.290 --> 00:01:11.020
And you get a consistent
operational experience

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:12.814
across all of your deployments.

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:17.520
And those are designed
to be deployed

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:20.460
wherever you deploy your apps.

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:22.530
You can use it to build
an application platform

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:27.090
on top that abstracts away
many platform specific details.

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:32.970
The vast majority
of enterprises today

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:37.740
are operating in a hybrid
or multi-cloud environment.

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:40.530
A lot of customers
want the flexibility

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:43.500
to choose different
environments for different apps

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:47.680
while reducing
operational overhead.

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:50.390
Some workloads must run
on prem due to security

00:01:50.390 --> 00:01:53.490
and regulatory reasons.

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:54.960
Other workloads
have been designed

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:57.630
to take advantage of some
best-of-breed solutions

00:01:57.630 --> 00:01:59.190
from specific cloud providers.

00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:05.620
And beyond that, many
customers want their apps

00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:08.259
to be able to run on
any cloud and sometimes

00:02:08.259 --> 00:02:11.080
be able to simultaneously
run on multiple clouds.

00:02:13.990 --> 00:02:16.030
Many customers want
to be able to burst

00:02:16.030 --> 00:02:19.120
from on prem to the cloud
or failover from one cloud

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:22.090
provider to another,
or load balance

00:02:22.090 --> 00:02:27.020
between multiple clouds,
and do all of this in order

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:30.740
to achieve resiliency
goals, cost savings,

00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:31.790
and reduce menu locking.

00:02:36.970 --> 00:02:39.940
To enable these hybrid
multi-cloud use cases,

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:43.860
Anthos is highly optimized for
many deployment environments

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:46.680
with a consistent
operational experience that

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:48.030
takes all of them into account.

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:54.400
On prem, we support
VM's running on VMware,

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:59.990
[INAUDIBLE] with Bare
Metal support coming soon.

00:02:59.990 --> 00:03:01.550
We interoperate
with a large number

00:03:01.550 --> 00:03:03.820
of storage and
networking providers

00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:06.890
so that the platform can easily
integrate with your existing

00:03:06.890 --> 00:03:07.958
infrastructure.

00:03:10.770 --> 00:03:14.050
In the cloud, we integrate
with cloud specific services,

00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:17.670
such as load balancers, private
virtual cloud, virtual climate

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:23.100
clouds, and
identity-and-access management.

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:26.950
GCP and AWS are currently
fully supported,

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:28.280
with Azure support coming soon.

00:03:31.390 --> 00:03:33.320
And at the mobile
edge, we're partnering

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:35.350
with telecommunications
companies

00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:37.340
and providing a
deployment option that's

00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:40.070
optimized for the small
footprint, large fleet

00:03:40.070 --> 00:03:42.740
size, and the unique
networking requirements

00:03:42.740 --> 00:03:45.260
of that environment.

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:48.400
Now, on top of this
distributed infrastructure,

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:50.790
Anthos provides the tooling
and the best practices

00:03:50.790 --> 00:03:55.550
built in for all the different
members of your team.

00:03:55.550 --> 00:03:59.480
For infrastructure operators,
we provide a unified experience

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:04.720
for managerial Kubernetes
clusters in your environments,

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:07.240
along with the configuration
policies required

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:09.490
to meet your enterprise
security and compliance needs.

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:15.100
For service operators,
we provide a way

00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:18.470
to gain visibility and
control of your services,

00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:22.180
including traffic management,
authentication authorization,

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:23.650
monitoring, and alerting.

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:30.740
And for developers, we provide
ways to easily deploy and run

00:04:30.740 --> 00:04:33.380
modern surveillance
workloads without needing

00:04:33.380 --> 00:04:35.724
to learn about the
underlying infrastructure.

00:04:38.630 --> 00:04:40.190
All of these
components are built

00:04:40.190 --> 00:04:42.800
on open source
foundations, much of which

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:45.470
was first developed at Google.

00:04:45.470 --> 00:04:48.760
With Anthos, we put the
LEGO pieces together

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:50.290
to give you a
managed and support

00:04:50.290 --> 00:04:53.290
experience for
your infrastructure

00:04:53.290 --> 00:04:56.330
so that you can focus on
your unique business logic.

00:04:56.330 --> 00:04:59.530
So let me show you in a demo a
bit more about how all of this

00:04:59.530 --> 00:05:00.960
works.

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:03.803
We'll first look at some details
of the individual clusters.

00:05:03.803 --> 00:05:05.970
And then we'll take a look
at the unified management

00:05:05.970 --> 00:05:09.230
experience provided on top.

00:05:09.230 --> 00:05:13.400
Here, I have created
four Anthos clusters.

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:18.010
The first one is a
GKE cluster on AWS.

00:05:18.010 --> 00:05:24.200
The second one is an
EKS cluster also on AWS.

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:26.835
And the bottom two are
GKE clusters on GCP.

00:05:30.310 --> 00:05:34.330
Anthos on AWS just reached
GA status recently.

00:05:34.330 --> 00:05:37.370
And we've heard of a lot
of customer interest here.

00:05:37.370 --> 00:05:41.440
So let's take a quick look at
how GKE integrates with the AWS

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:42.312
environment.

00:05:45.930 --> 00:05:48.390
Under the hood, the cluster
is running as a number

00:05:48.390 --> 00:05:51.735
of [INAUDIBLE] VM's in AWS.

00:05:51.735 --> 00:05:55.500
A cluster control plane
consists of three VM's that can

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:58.320
be deployed in a highly
available fashion, as we

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:00.990
have it here, across
three availability

00:06:00.990 --> 00:06:02.250
zones in the same region.

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:08.860
The other three VM's here
belong to a single node pool

00:06:08.860 --> 00:06:12.520
deployed in a single
availability zone.

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:14.657
You can also add in
additional node pools

00:06:14.657 --> 00:06:16.240
and put them in
different availability

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:17.410
zones in that same region.

00:06:22.310 --> 00:06:27.160
Note here that none of these
nodes have public IP addresses.

00:06:27.160 --> 00:06:29.500
For security reasons,
most of our customers

00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:32.410
prefer that the clusters
are available only

00:06:32.410 --> 00:06:34.720
on an internal
network and can only

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:39.860
be accessed via a VPN
or a similar mechanism.

00:06:39.860 --> 00:06:41.970
A lot of customers
have well-established

00:06:41.970 --> 00:06:47.270
AWS VPC's that have been through
rigorous security reviews.

00:06:47.270 --> 00:06:50.660
So GKE is designed to be able
to install into the customer's

00:06:50.660 --> 00:06:55.200
own VPC and stay within
the established boundaries.

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:57.090
From a management
perspective, we're

00:06:57.090 --> 00:07:00.300
starting with the foundation
that most customers are already

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:01.940
familiar with.

00:07:01.940 --> 00:07:04.040
Just like the
Kubernetes-based apps

00:07:04.040 --> 00:07:07.970
that you deploy and manage,
GKE clusters and other Anthos

00:07:07.970 --> 00:07:11.680
resources can also be
defined declaratively

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:15.020
in a YAML file which can be
stored in source control.

00:07:15.020 --> 00:07:18.650
And they can follow similar
processes like code reviews

00:07:18.650 --> 00:07:22.940
and have manual approvals
before deployment.

00:07:22.940 --> 00:07:25.130
Additionally, you
can enforce policies

00:07:25.130 --> 00:07:27.470
on it using the
same tools that you

00:07:27.470 --> 00:07:29.900
use to enforce
policies on apps that

00:07:29.900 --> 00:07:33.580
define [INAUDIBLE] YAML files.

00:07:33.580 --> 00:07:36.490
For example, here
is the YAML file

00:07:36.490 --> 00:07:40.930
I used to define the GKE
cluster on AWS that we saw.

00:07:43.620 --> 00:07:45.660
We defined here what
region the clusters

00:07:45.660 --> 00:07:55.810
should run in, what VPC to use,
what subnets to use, and so on.

00:07:55.810 --> 00:07:58.660
With this, customers
can then layer policies

00:07:58.660 --> 00:08:02.960
on top such as to enforce
that only certain VPC's

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:06.930
may be used for GKE clusters.

00:08:06.930 --> 00:08:09.530
Now, zooming out a
bit, we've just briefly

00:08:09.530 --> 00:08:13.400
explored how GKE integrates
with the native environment,

00:08:13.400 --> 00:08:16.910
specifically in the
case of running on AWS.

00:08:16.910 --> 00:08:18.980
You may be wondering
how we provide

00:08:18.980 --> 00:08:21.620
a unified operational
experience on top

00:08:21.620 --> 00:08:24.110
of such a large variety
of cluster deployment

00:08:24.110 --> 00:08:25.960
environments.

00:08:25.960 --> 00:08:28.780
That journey starts with
a unified authentication

00:08:28.780 --> 00:08:31.010
experience.

00:08:31.010 --> 00:08:35.370
Many customers have standardized
on a few top identity providers

00:08:35.370 --> 00:08:38.120
for their corporate
authentication needs,

00:08:38.120 --> 00:08:43.750
such as Microsoft Active
Directory, Okta, and others.

00:08:43.750 --> 00:08:48.230
GKE clusters support
OIDC, Open ID Connect,

00:08:48.230 --> 00:08:50.270
so that customers
can authenticate

00:08:50.270 --> 00:08:53.420
using the same familiar
corporate credentials to all

00:08:53.420 --> 00:08:55.670
their clusters across
all their deployment

00:08:55.670 --> 00:08:58.010
environments,
instead of using GCP

00:08:58.010 --> 00:09:01.010
accounts for some clusters, AWS
accounts for other clusters,

00:09:01.010 --> 00:09:02.950
and so on.

00:09:02.950 --> 00:09:05.830
Let's see how this works.

00:09:05.830 --> 00:09:11.060
Here, I've registered
my GKE on AWS cluster.

00:09:11.060 --> 00:09:14.332
But I haven't yet
authenticated to it.

00:09:14.332 --> 00:09:15.540
Let's click the Login button.

00:09:25.060 --> 00:09:28.320
In this case, we're
using Okta as our example

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:30.800
corporate identity provider.

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:32.770
So I'll enter in my
Okta credentials here.

00:09:53.860 --> 00:09:57.280
And now I've logged in.

00:09:57.280 --> 00:09:59.720
The process is similar
on the command line

00:09:59.720 --> 00:10:04.310
and also works the same way
for other Anthos clusters.

00:10:04.310 --> 00:10:06.800
After authentication,
I can now use

00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:09.890
this web console to see
information about the cluster.

00:10:17.990 --> 00:10:25.370
I can see the nodes that make
up the cluster and information

00:10:25.370 --> 00:10:29.120
about each node, such as the
pods running on that node.

00:10:32.990 --> 00:10:37.520
Now, so far, we've explored
running GKE on AWS.

00:10:37.520 --> 00:10:40.460
That's one option for
running your workloads.

00:10:40.460 --> 00:10:45.450
You can also run GKE on other
clouds or in your own data set.

00:10:45.450 --> 00:10:49.990
You can attach your own existing
EKS or AKS cluster to Anthos.

00:10:49.990 --> 00:10:52.350
You'll soon be able to bring
VM's to Anthos as well.

00:10:55.580 --> 00:10:59.530
For example, the second cluster
I have here in the web console

00:10:59.530 --> 00:11:03.070
is an AWS EKS cluster
that's been registered

00:11:03.070 --> 00:11:06.280
to my Anthos [INAUDIBLE],,
an administrative domain

00:11:06.280 --> 00:11:09.300
for Anthos resources.

00:11:09.300 --> 00:11:12.740
Once registered, you can run
the same higher level Anthos

00:11:12.740 --> 00:11:16.020
services on top, such as
configuration and policy

00:11:16.020 --> 00:11:20.490
management, as well
as service management.

00:11:20.490 --> 00:11:22.860
With this option,
customers can keep

00:11:22.860 --> 00:11:25.760
using their existing
Kubernetes distribution

00:11:25.760 --> 00:11:28.800
and can more easily
onboard onto Anthos

00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:30.990
while relying on Google
to test and support

00:11:30.990 --> 00:11:34.370
the integrated solution.

00:11:34.370 --> 00:11:38.330
AWS, EKS, and Azure AKS
are supported today.

00:11:38.330 --> 00:11:42.350
And more distributions will
be supported in the future.

00:11:42.350 --> 00:11:45.160
Now, if and when you'd
like to move the GKE,

00:11:45.160 --> 00:11:47.530
you'll get many
additional benefits.

00:11:47.530 --> 00:11:49.570
You'll get a
Kubernetes distribution

00:11:49.570 --> 00:11:51.780
backed by the company
that created Kubernetes

00:11:51.780 --> 00:11:54.940
and with the most
experience in managing it.

00:11:54.940 --> 00:11:57.070
It'll be the same
Kubernetes distribution

00:11:57.070 --> 00:12:01.120
everywhere with same networking
and identity [INAUDIBLE],, many

00:12:01.120 --> 00:12:04.610
of the same security
features and so on.

00:12:04.610 --> 00:12:06.620
You'll get a more
consistent experience

00:12:06.620 --> 00:12:09.070
for managing the cluster
in its lifecycle.

00:12:12.150 --> 00:12:14.570
Now, switching
gears, just seeing

00:12:14.570 --> 00:12:18.455
how Anthos provides a consistent
management experience to access

00:12:18.455 --> 00:12:23.290
all of my clusters across
deployment environments,

00:12:23.290 --> 00:12:26.170
beyond access,
many customers also

00:12:26.170 --> 00:12:29.950
need a way to ensure that their
policies and configuration can

00:12:29.950 --> 00:12:35.110
be applied across all clusters
reliably and uniformly.

00:12:35.110 --> 00:12:38.170
And this is where ACM, Anthos
Configuration Management,

00:12:38.170 --> 00:12:38.670
comes in.

00:12:41.290 --> 00:12:44.740
With ACM, you can have all
of your cluster configuration

00:12:44.740 --> 00:12:48.740
policies defined in a
central Git repository.

00:12:48.740 --> 00:12:50.740
And that will be
synchronized and applied

00:12:50.740 --> 00:12:54.090
across all of your
Anthos clusters.

00:12:54.090 --> 00:12:56.850
This gives you a way to easily
manage a fleet of clusters

00:12:56.850 --> 00:13:01.300
and prevent drift with
continuous monitoring.

00:13:01.300 --> 00:13:03.520
Using git as the
source of truth,

00:13:03.520 --> 00:13:06.490
you can manage your
configuration policies as code

00:13:06.490 --> 00:13:09.110
with the power of
source control,

00:13:09.110 --> 00:13:11.860
so you can integrate
code review flows,

00:13:11.860 --> 00:13:15.435
be able to easily audit changes,
and you can easily revert back

00:13:15.435 --> 00:13:16.310
to previous versions.

00:13:19.050 --> 00:13:22.620
Many of our customers use ACM
to apply and enforce policies

00:13:22.620 --> 00:13:26.520
like limiting host OS
access, as well as limiting

00:13:26.520 --> 00:13:29.310
container refill
access, and shutting off

00:13:29.310 --> 00:13:30.770
external [INAUDIBLE].

00:13:35.470 --> 00:13:37.750
For this demo, I've
configured all four

00:13:37.750 --> 00:13:40.300
of my clusters to
pull the configuration

00:13:40.300 --> 00:13:44.075
policies from the git repo.

00:13:44.075 --> 00:13:45.950
They're all pulling from
the same git branch.

00:13:45.950 --> 00:13:48.070
And they've all updated
to the latest commit.

00:13:55.390 --> 00:14:06.183
Clicking to one of them, the
git repo URL is configured here.

00:14:06.183 --> 00:14:08.350
And the subdirectory we're
using is configured here.

00:14:11.560 --> 00:14:27.730
If I navigate to this git repo,
and then the subdirectory,

00:14:27.730 --> 00:14:29.770
I can see the
configuration and policies

00:14:29.770 --> 00:14:32.680
that are being applied.

00:14:32.680 --> 00:14:35.620
Specifically, in this
cluster directory,

00:14:35.620 --> 00:14:37.780
I have a cluster y to
configuration in policies.

00:14:42.300 --> 00:14:44.900
For example, we
have a policy here

00:14:44.900 --> 00:14:47.660
that requires that all
namespaces have a cost center

00:14:47.660 --> 00:14:49.260
label for auditing purposes.

00:14:54.810 --> 00:14:58.170
Going back, there is also
a namespaces directory

00:14:58.170 --> 00:15:01.620
that defines a few named places
that will be managed by ACM.

00:15:05.460 --> 00:15:09.058
For example, in this file, we
create a shipping Dev namespace

00:15:09.058 --> 00:15:10.100
with a cost center label.

00:15:14.950 --> 00:15:23.860
Switching back to
the terminal, we

00:15:23.860 --> 00:15:27.040
see that the shipping Dev name
face has been created by ACM.

00:15:31.030 --> 00:15:33.580
Let's see what happens if we
try to delete that namespace.

00:15:43.080 --> 00:15:45.170
We see that the namespace
was deleted and then

00:15:45.170 --> 00:15:49.670
immediately recreated by
ACM about three seconds ago.

00:15:49.670 --> 00:15:53.180
So ACM is constantly
monitoring to make sure

00:15:53.180 --> 00:15:55.950
that what's deployed matches
the source of truth in your git

00:15:55.950 --> 00:15:56.450
repo.

00:15:59.440 --> 00:16:01.960
Now, what if we were
to try to something

00:16:01.960 --> 00:16:04.710
that violates our policies?

00:16:04.710 --> 00:16:06.990
We saw earlier that
there's a policy requiring

00:16:06.990 --> 00:16:08.970
that all namespaces have
a call center label.

00:16:12.100 --> 00:16:15.430
If we try to create a namespace
that doesn't have such a label,

00:16:15.430 --> 00:16:16.580
this action is denied.

00:16:16.580 --> 00:16:18.205
And the namespace
does not get created.

00:16:26.800 --> 00:16:30.080
Without cluster configuration
and policies in place,

00:16:30.080 --> 00:16:34.490
the next thing we might want to
set up is our CI/CD pipeline.

00:16:34.490 --> 00:16:37.310
For this, one simple
option is to use

00:16:37.310 --> 00:16:41.660
Google Cloud Build, our fully
managed, serverless CI/CD

00:16:41.660 --> 00:16:43.950
platform.

00:16:43.950 --> 00:16:46.240
With Google Cloud
Build, you can easily

00:16:46.240 --> 00:16:49.740
find pipelines consisting of
arbitrated built steps run

00:16:49.740 --> 00:16:51.970
as container images.

00:16:51.970 --> 00:16:55.080
They can be automatically
triggered from your source code

00:16:55.080 --> 00:17:00.520
repository and run on
Google's infrastructure.

00:17:00.520 --> 00:17:04.270
For this demo, I set a
really simple pipeline here

00:17:04.270 --> 00:17:07.430
that performs three steps.

00:17:07.430 --> 00:17:12.900
First, we do a Git clone of
the Kubernetes examples repo.

00:17:12.900 --> 00:17:15.150
Then we update the
service definition

00:17:15.150 --> 00:17:18.480
to use a service of type load
balancer instead of node port.

00:17:21.810 --> 00:17:24.910
And finally, we
configure [INAUDIBLE]

00:17:24.910 --> 00:17:27.040
and use it to deploy the
Guestbook sample app.

00:17:29.760 --> 00:17:31.614
Now let's kick off
the deployment.

00:17:39.200 --> 00:17:41.610
While that's running, let's
view the progress in the web

00:17:41.610 --> 00:17:42.110
console.

00:17:52.250 --> 00:17:55.850
Here, we can see the three steps
and the output from each step.

00:18:08.140 --> 00:18:10.390
Notice that we deployed
this app to our Anthos

00:18:10.390 --> 00:18:13.540
GKE on AWS cluster.

00:18:13.540 --> 00:18:17.730
So while your CICD pipeline
can be running on Google Cloud,

00:18:17.730 --> 00:18:20.610
you can use it to deploy
to any Anthos cluster,

00:18:20.610 --> 00:18:23.400
including clusters on
Prem and in other clouds.

00:18:26.210 --> 00:18:28.240
In this case, our
cluster is actually

00:18:28.240 --> 00:18:32.390
sitting in a private VPC in AWS.

00:18:32.390 --> 00:18:35.810
We're able to access it because
there's an agent running

00:18:35.810 --> 00:18:38.200
in that cluster that
reaches outbound

00:18:38.200 --> 00:18:42.390
a Google Cloud to maintain
a persistent connection.

00:18:42.390 --> 00:18:44.550
We didn't need to
open up any firewalls

00:18:44.550 --> 00:18:49.340
or expose any public IP
addresses for that cluster.

00:18:49.340 --> 00:18:51.710
Visibility to proxy of
Kubernetes control plane

00:18:51.710 --> 00:18:53.540
traffic is currently in alpha.

00:18:57.340 --> 00:18:58.980
So this pipeline
is pretty simple.

00:18:58.980 --> 00:19:01.800
And it finished quickly.

00:19:01.800 --> 00:19:04.690
You can, of course,
include many more steps,

00:19:04.690 --> 00:19:07.570
such as steps that build
container images, run

00:19:07.570 --> 00:19:10.240
validation, and
unit tests, and so

00:19:10.240 --> 00:19:13.730
on all within this
fully managed service.

00:19:16.320 --> 00:19:20.503
Let's now move to the Kubernetes
page in the web console.

00:19:20.503 --> 00:19:21.920
We can select the
cluster to view.

00:19:27.350 --> 00:19:29.940
And we see here that
our guestbook workload

00:19:29.940 --> 00:19:32.680
has been deployed.

00:19:32.680 --> 00:19:36.630
In addition, our guestbook
service is also running

00:19:36.630 --> 00:19:43.870
and an AWS ELB, Elastic Load
Balancer, has been allocated.

00:19:43.870 --> 00:19:51.900
We can click into that, view the
guestbook, which is currently

00:19:51.900 --> 00:19:57.130
empty, and add an entry to it.

00:19:57.130 --> 00:19:59.270
With this Cloud
build integration,

00:19:59.270 --> 00:20:02.360
you can build your app once
using our serverless CI/CD

00:20:02.360 --> 00:20:03.430
platform.

00:20:03.430 --> 00:20:06.430
And deploy it across
multiple clouds.

00:20:06.430 --> 00:20:08.560
For customers that
need to build jobs

00:20:08.560 --> 00:20:11.110
to be able to access
private resources,

00:20:11.110 --> 00:20:13.390
or need more
configurability, we're

00:20:13.390 --> 00:20:17.800
also offering a new feature
called custom worker pools,

00:20:17.800 --> 00:20:20.250
currently unlimited
beta, where you

00:20:20.250 --> 00:20:22.140
can run builds in
your private network

00:20:22.140 --> 00:20:25.415
on GCP using VPC
network [INAUDIBLE]..

00:20:29.060 --> 00:20:31.310
Now, after we've
deployed our apps,

00:20:31.310 --> 00:20:33.200
one more thing we
might want to do

00:20:33.200 --> 00:20:35.740
is to be able to monitor them.

00:20:35.740 --> 00:20:40.360
Anthos makes this easy with
ASM, Anthos Service Mesh, which

00:20:40.360 --> 00:20:43.120
is our managed version of
the open source [INAUDIBLE]

00:20:43.120 --> 00:20:45.280
project.

00:20:45.280 --> 00:20:49.450
With ASM, service operators
can gain more visibility

00:20:49.450 --> 00:20:53.860
and control over your services,
including traffic management,

00:20:53.860 --> 00:20:57.298
authentication and
authorization, monitoring,

00:20:57.298 --> 00:20:57.840
and alerting.

00:21:02.990 --> 00:21:06.800
For this demo, I deployed a
sample online boutique app

00:21:06.800 --> 00:21:12.000
a few days ago on my
two GKE clusters on GCP.

00:21:12.000 --> 00:21:16.530
With the ASMUI, we can see at a
glance how all of our services

00:21:16.530 --> 00:21:18.700
are doing.

00:21:18.700 --> 00:21:20.873
We can also drop
into a topology view

00:21:20.873 --> 00:21:23.040
to see how the services
communicate with each other.

00:21:29.070 --> 00:21:31.900
From here, we can
drill into each service

00:21:31.900 --> 00:21:33.820
to get more information
about that service.

00:21:39.530 --> 00:21:42.870
We can see any SLO's
we set for the service,

00:21:42.870 --> 00:21:46.010
as well as a lot of
metrics for the service,

00:21:46.010 --> 00:21:56.220
such as requests per second,
error rates, latency, request

00:21:56.220 --> 00:21:57.360
size, and so on.

00:22:00.260 --> 00:22:04.310
As of the latest release,
GKE clusters on GCP

00:22:04.310 --> 00:22:06.980
can be part of the
same service mesh.

00:22:06.980 --> 00:22:09.410
So they can load
balance between them.

00:22:09.410 --> 00:22:11.450
And we can aggregate
to the metrics

00:22:11.450 --> 00:22:14.770
for the services
running on them.

00:22:14.770 --> 00:22:18.170
So the metrics we see here are
aggregated from both of our GKE

00:22:18.170 --> 00:22:19.730
clusters that are on GCP.

00:22:23.420 --> 00:22:26.750
If we want, we can also
break this down by cluster

00:22:26.750 --> 00:22:30.130
to monitor each
customer separately.

00:22:30.130 --> 00:22:33.270
For example, let's do that
for this latency graph here.

00:22:37.920 --> 00:22:41.370
With this view, we can see how
the services in each cluster

00:22:41.370 --> 00:22:44.200
perform relative to each other.

00:22:44.200 --> 00:22:46.400
The red line
represents one cluster.

00:22:46.400 --> 00:22:50.050
And the blue line
represents the other.

00:22:50.050 --> 00:22:54.100
Taken together, these monitoring
and multi-cluster capabilities

00:22:54.100 --> 00:22:58.890
make ASM a great tool for
operating your services.

00:22:58.890 --> 00:23:01.310
Note, though, that these
capabilities are currently

00:23:01.310 --> 00:23:04.880
only available for
GKE clusters on GCP

00:23:04.880 --> 00:23:06.860
and will be coming
soon to Anthos clusters

00:23:06.860 --> 00:23:08.860
running on prem and
on other clouds.

00:23:12.050 --> 00:23:14.450
To wrap up, we just
saw in the demo

00:23:14.450 --> 00:23:17.960
today how we can bring workloads
running on other clouds

00:23:17.960 --> 00:23:22.610
to Anthos, whether you choose
to use GKE or another Kurbenetes

00:23:22.610 --> 00:23:25.380
distribution.

00:23:25.380 --> 00:23:28.440
We saw a unified authentication
and management experience

00:23:28.440 --> 00:23:31.130
from the web console.

00:23:31.130 --> 00:23:33.320
We applied the same
configuration policies

00:23:33.320 --> 00:23:36.300
to all of our clusters.

00:23:36.300 --> 00:23:38.310
We deployed an app
to a GKE cluster

00:23:38.310 --> 00:23:43.060
on AWS using Google Cloud Build.

00:23:43.060 --> 00:23:45.310
And we saw how we can
use Anthos service

00:23:45.310 --> 00:23:50.310
mesh to monitor the services
running in our clusters.

00:23:50.310 --> 00:23:53.760
There's a lot more we didn't
have a chance to cover today.

00:23:53.760 --> 00:23:56.030
So check out the other
sessions this week,

00:23:56.030 --> 00:24:01.790
as well as our website at
cloud.google.com/anthos.

00:24:01.790 --> 00:24:05.360
Thanks for watching,
and stay safe.

